@@ -153,6 +153,46 @@ Every configured prefix enters the same prefix mode and starts every `prefix+...
 
 The default keymap is prefix-first so Herdr does not steal input from shells, editors, tmux, or terminal apps. Search `keys.` in the [Config reference](/docs/config-reference/) to see every action and default binding. The in-app help panel at `prefix+?` shows the active bindings.
 
+Press **prefix then o** to start your preferred agent immediately in the selected pane's
+existing shell and current working directory. Use an available shell prompt;
+Herdr refuses a managed-agent pane, a non-shell process, or a shell with running
+child processes instead of sending launch text to that application. This uses the
+same managed launch as new-tab auto-start. No shell alias or profile setup is needed.
+**prefix then Shift+O** opens the visible notification's target.
+
+```toml
+[keys]
+start_agent = "prefix+o"
+open_notification_target = "prefix+shift+o"
+
+[agent]
+kind = "opencode"
+args = []
+```
+
+These are client keybindings. With the default local keybindings for a remote
+connection, configure them once on the host client; the action targets the selected
+remote pane. Existing server-keybinding mode still uses the server's key profile.
+Client and server must both support this shortcut: an older server without the
+advertised `agent.start` method shows an unavailable-action notice and receives no
+terminal input. Explicit older key overrides remain in effect until you change them.
+
+`[agent]` always comes from the client configuration, even when server keybindings
+are selected. Choose a supported agent such as `opencode`, `codex`, or `claude`.
+The corresponding program must be installed on the selected server. `args` is a
+list of arguments, for example `["--model", "your-model"]`, not an executable,
+shell command, alias, or pipeline. Herdr renders those arguments for its supported
+PowerShell, cmd, Nushell and POSIX shell paths; do not add shell quoting yourself.
+For OpenCode, Herdr retains the ephemeral loopback HTTP flags needed for child
+session splits even with additional arguments. Explicit hostname, port and mDNS
+options in `agent.args` are rejected rather than overriding those owned flags.
+
+The former development-only `keys.start_opencode` field is replaced by
+`keys.start_agent`; update an explicit override once. The default remains
+`prefix+o`. Automatic new-tab launch stays separately opt-in through
+`session.auto_start_agent` on the server; selecting a preferred client agent does
+not enable auto-start or change existing sessions.
+
 A binding may also be an array when one action needs multiple shortcuts:
 
 ```toml
@@ -546,6 +586,17 @@ The legacy `experimental.kitty_graphics` setting remains accepted for existing c
 
 Changing this setting requires restarting the affected Herdr server or reattaching the client. In remote sessions, the server's setting controls Kitty graphics parsing, while the local client's setting controls output to the outer terminal.
 
+## Start an Agent in every new tab
+
+To start one supported interactive Agent in the root pane of every genuinely new tab in a persistent session, opt in with its `herdr agent start --kind` label:
+
+```toml
+[session]
+auto_start_agent = "opencode"
+```
+
+The setting is unset by default. It applies to the initial tab of a new persistent session and later tabs created through the UI, CLI, or API. When live reload enables or changes the setting, Herdr also starts the selected Agent in each existing tab root that is currently waiting at its shell. It does not run automatically for restored or reattached tabs at startup, live handoff, `--no-session`, or split panes. Auto-start uses the normal managed Agent path, so exiting or interrupting the Agent returns to the tab's underlying shell. Herdr reads this value from your config and never adds or rewrites it.
+
 ## Agent session restore
 
 Herdr resumes supported Agent conversations after a server restart by default:
